  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA warm front will continue to lift through the region early this \nmorning with the first round of showers that are expected today. \nElevated instability will support isolated heavier showers and \npotentially some non-severe thunderstorms as well. Despite strong \nflow aloft, an inversion will limit the mixing potential tonight. \nHowever, gusts over 25 mph will still be possible.\n\nThis first round of showers will be through by the mid-morning \nhours, though some spotty showers will still be possible given the \nclose proximity of the associated surface low. A squall line is \nexpected to develop along a cold front that drops in from the \nnorthwest by midday and quickly moves through during the afternoon \nhours. While the region will be in the warm sector prior to the \narrival of the front, there is uncertainty on how much instability \nwill develop under a weak October sun angle and limited clearing of \nskies. Models continue to show SBCAPE value of less than 500 J/kg. \nStrong mid-level flow will lead to 0-6 km shear rising to 50 to 60 \nkts - a typical low CAPE, high shear environment for this region. \nBased on the timing of the front, the best chance for strong to \nsevere thunderstorms will be across Northeast PA and the Catskills. \nThe main hazard will be strong wind gusts. Low-level shear is also \nquite high, which supports a low potential for a spin up or two but \nthat will depend on having adequate instability.\n\nFollowing the cold front, northwesterly flow will advect in much \ncolder air. Temperatures will drop into the 20s and 30s tonight. \nLake effect precipitation will develop late today and be enhanced by \na passing upper-level trough. Temperatures will generally stay warm \nenough for just rain though a rain/snow mix or even a brief period \nof just snow overnight will be possible, especially over the Finger \nLakes Region. Little to no snowfall accumulation is expected. High \npressure builds into the region Saturday, eventually cutting off the \nlake effect precipitation by the afternoon. Northwesterly flow may \nkeep the clouds around slightly longer than forecasted, but some \nlate breaks of sunshine are expected. Saturday will be quite chilly \nwith highs only in the 40s. Then overnight, clear skies will lead to \nradiational cooling and temperatures dropping into the 20s. For the \nWyoming Valley, frost/freeze headlines will likely be needed.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe area of high pressure will remain present over the region \nthroughout next week. A quasi-stationary low pressure system will \ndevelop along the East Coast. At times, it will drift northward but \nthe high pressure will keep precipitation out of the region. \nTemperatures will gradually warm up into early next week, becoming \nmore seasonable. Sunny skies may help push high temperatures back \ninto the 70s in the valleys.",
